A||N||13||trigger||steering column lock and other little things||||1||
Z||000000||trigger||08-09-2000||05:16 PM||||I was wondering what you had to do to get the steering column lock.  I drive a 88 coupe that doesn't have it, but my mechanics 84 SE parts car does.  Also he has another 84 parts car that has a Fiero GT steering wheel so does the SE.  Did somebody add those or did they come with it?  Also, what year did Pontiac stop using those black wheels with the gold "veins" on the Fiero?  Thanks for any help<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.<p>[This message has been edited by trigger (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||08-10-2000||02:01 AM||||You've got me thoroughly confused.  What steering column lock are you referring to?  If you're talking about being able to lock the wheel (keep it from turning) with the key, they all came that way.  If yours isn't like that, either something's broken or somethings definitely not stock.<P>I don't remember any black wheels with gold "veins".  If you're referring to the GT/lace wheels, they were either black or gold (or gray) but not any combination of those.||||reg||1
Z||000002||trigger||08-10-2000||01:25 PM||||The veined wheels are hard to describe.  They were different than the GT rims.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||08-10-2000||04:44 PM||||steering lock...... do you mean the lock that keeps you from turning the steering wheel? <P>or the little button that keeps you from taking out the key? Stick cars have a button for that but automatics use a cable to the shifter. If you put an automatic column in a stick car you lose that function completely.||||reg||1
Z||000004||trigger||08-10-2000||06:08 PM||||I'm talking about the lock that keeps you from turning the wheel.  Thanks.<P>------------------<BR>Honestly officer, I was just keeping up with the flow of traffic.  I know there aren't any cars in the area, that's how far behind I am.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||08-11-2000||01:34 AM||||Can you post a pic of the wheels you're talking about?  As far as I know, the Fiero only had 4 wheel options.  13" steel with hubcaps, 13" finned ('84 only?), 14" Tech style (common on the old Grand Ams, too), and the 15" GT/laced wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||08-11-2000||10:00 AM||||<BR>No flame intended for MM, but the wheels on the Grand Ams, Sunbirds and such are not exactly the same as on the Fiero.<P>There is a distinctive lip to the Fiero wheel, which is almost absent on the Grand Am wheel.<P>The wheel on the Fiero is designed with an offset to mimic a rear wheel drive car, similiar to the Firebird wheel it was patterned after.  The offset on the front drive Grand Am is less to allow the wheel to fit under the fender.<P>The Hi-Tech style wheel is a popular design for many of the Pontiac products of the 80's.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||08-11-2000||11:03 AM||||Like I said, ...14" Tech <B>style</B>...<P>I didn't say that the wheel was identical to the ones on the Grand Ams.  I just wanted him to know what style I was referring to.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||08-11-2000||11:42 AM||||All GM steering columns since the early 70's are built with a rotation lock. Only about 3 things will disable it.<P>1. someone took off the steering wheel and didn't replace the lock plate when they where done doing whatever they where doing.<P>2. The spring on the lock pin broke. This spring is what sets the pin when the key is out.<P>3. there is to much dirt on/in the pin for it to slide free and lock.<P>you'll need to pull the wheel to find out what's up. If it's the pin try hitting it with a little WD40 to free it up before you mess with the lock plate.||||reg||1
Z||000009||trigger||08-11-2000||12:38 PM||||I was talking about the "finned" rim, I guess.  I never seen them up close.  What did they come on?  Can you describe the pin a little more closely on the steering wheel lock and the plate?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||08-11-2000||01:40 PM||||take off the steering wheel and remove the plastic cover under it. The lock plate is the next layer.<P>The pin is behind the plate in about the 1-2 oclock position. The pin pops up against the plate or into the plate slots when the key is taken out.||||reg||1
Z||000011||trigger||08-11-2000||06:12 PM||||Okay, I took off the steering wheel and the platic cover and worked with the key and pin.  I sprayed it pretty good with WD-40 and still no luck.  Once in a while when you turn the ingnition of the pin will come up about halfway and then it falls back down.  What should I try now?  How do you get at the spring?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-11-2000||10:52 PM||||The spring is shot. leave it alone. You have to tear the column pretty far apart to get to it as I recall. It's made intentionally dificult to mess with so it won't get removed.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||08-12-2000||01:43 AM||||The finned rims are 13" diameter.  I thought they were only available on the '84s, but I just received a dealer catalog thing (you know, the 6 page brochure that the dealer keeps in the rack for each vehicle) for the '85s and it was an option then, too.  Tough to get performance tires for a 13" rim.  In order to stay away from the rollerskate look you'd have tons of extra room in your wheel wells.  You can get the 14" Hi-Tech rims dirt cheap and there are lots of stock (but not for the Fiero) 15" rims out there that can be had for a song.||||reg||1
